      "visible": true,
      "showLabel": false,
      "data": {
        "title": "Semantic Zoom Has No Middle Ground",
        "statement": "Twilite has four mechanisms that each get called \"semantic zoom\": a node's content scales with the camera, a node picks between its own detail/summary/icon views, a whole unfocused graph in the workspace collapses to a level band by how much screen it occupies, and a view sequence gives the fallback order between named views. None of them reflows a layout. A graph authored with roomy spacing shows the same proportion of empty canvas at every zoom level, all the way until the workspace band system replaces the entire graph with a single icon. There is nothing between \"full detail, sparse\" and \"one icon.\"",
        "status": "framed",
        "confidence": "unproven",
        "notes": "Raised from a real screenshot: the Node Class Template, opened directly, mostly empty canvas at a low zoom. Measured: its nodes cover 22.7% of their own bounding box. But the deeper finding is that none of the four zoom mechanisms would have helped here even if tuned differently, because the one that is supposed to help (the workspace band system) does not apply to a focused graph at all.",
        "definitionKey": "idea",
