      ],
      "handles": [
        {
          "id": "root",
          "key": "root",
          "label": "root",
          "direction": "input",
          "dataType": "any",
          "angle": 270,
          "portId": "root"
        },
        {
          "id": "surface-delegate",
          "key": "surface-delegate",
          "portId": "surface-delegate",
          "label": "surface delegate",
          "direction": "output",
          "dataType": "any",
          "angle": 90,
          "role": "view.content"
        }
      ],
      "visible": true,
      "showLabel": true,
      "data": {
        "view": {
          "intent": "node",
          "payload": "node.web.detail"
        },
        "semanticLevel": "detail",
        "content": {
          "kind": "markdown",
          "value": "# Semantic Zoom Has No Middle Ground\n\nTwilite has four mechanisms that each get called \"semantic zoom,\" and none of them shares a model with the others, or ever repacks a layout. A node's content scales with the camera (nodeContentScale.js), a node picks between its own detail/summary/icon views (data._semanticLevel), a whole unfocused graph in the workspace collapses to a level band by screen occupancy (graphViewPresentation.js, graphWorkspace.js), and a view sequence gives the fallback order between named views on its own resolution scale (viewSequence.js). Because none of them ever moves a node's position, a graph authored with comfortable spacing looks the same fraction empty at every zoom, right up until the band system, which only applies to unfocused graphs, replaces the whole thing with one icon.\n\nMeasured on the Node Class Template: nodes cover 22.7% of their own 5500 by 3040 bounding box. And the screenshot that raised this was of a *focused* graph, where the band system that is supposed to manage this never runs at all — `presentWorkspaceInstances` always returns `expanded` for the focused instance.\n\nThe proposed approach treats this as two separable pieces: first, name one threshold model the four mechanisms read instead of keeping four vocabularies (an occupancy percent, a 0-3 resolution, a level string, a raw zoom number) — mechanical, low-risk, the same medicine as the wheel contract. Second, and only once that is settled, decide on purpose whether any level of that model may repack positions to fit the room it has, since letting a canvas move things without being asked is a real design decision, not a wiring cleanup, and zoomable-interface precedent (Pad++, space-scale diagrams) generally keeps position fixed across scale for exactly that reason."
        },
        "interfaceContract": {
          "version": 1,
          "ownsContent": true,
          "surfaceDelegation": true
        },
        "identity": {
          "graphId": "semantic-zoom-architecture"
        }
      }
    },
