      "data": {
        "title": "If positions compact as the view gets smaller, people still recognize the graph when they zoom back in",
        "statement": "Zoomable-interface research (Pad++, space-scale diagrams) generally treats position as fixed and only swaps appearance or level of detail; genuine reflow-on-zoom is uncommon for canvas tools precisely because spatial constancy is usually the point of a canvas. It is not yet known whether Twilite's graphs, which are read for their structure more than their exact placement, are an exception. — Moot: reflow was rejected outright (see the idea), so this never needs to be tested.",
        "importance": "high",
        "status": "moot",
