      "visible": true,
      "showLabel": false,
      "data": {
        "title": "Is \"expanded\" supposed to mean untouched, or does it need its own bottom band?",
        "question": "Right now focusing a graph always shows it at full, raw detail regardless of how zoomed out the camera is. Is that intentional (focus means \"see everything, unmanaged\"), or should a very sparse focused graph get some form of compaction or fit-to-content too?",
        "status": "open",
        "answer": "Leaning yes, per Michael: a focused graph should be able to unfocus at a low enough resolution, the same way an unfocused graph collapses today, rather than staying expanded forever. Likely shape: it is a graph-level zoom, not a per-node one — zooming out far enough on a focused graph returns it to its own summary or icon view. The exact trigger, and how the transition should feel, are still being worked out (see the reworked prototype next step).",
        "definitionKey": "idea-question",
        "_classBinding": {
          "key": "idea-question",
